[
  "ACT 1: COLD OPEN & PERSONAL GROUNDING [00:00 - 03:00]\nScene: 01. Starting Soon -> 02. Monologue / Full Camera\n\nWelcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall, and this is Episode 2. Today we return to SCNA 2011 at the Swissotel in Chicago for my conversation with Ray Hightower, the founder of WisdomGroup and organizer of ChicagoRuby and WindyCityRails. If Sergio Pereira gave me my first user group room in the Sears Tower, Ray Hightower was the ambassador who opened the door to the entire Chicago Ruby and software craftsmanship community.\n",
  "ACT 2: SETTING THE HISTORICAL SCENE [03:00 - 08:00]\nScene: 02. Monologue / Full Camera\n\nIn late 2008, I was working remotely as Principal Developer at Business Decisions, Inc., maintaining a multi-tenant HR platform and coordinating with developers in Uruguay. I reached out to Ray, and we met up halfway at Midway Restaurant. I was still a Microsoft .NET developer at the time. Ray did not gatekeep or lecture me about platforms. He just opened his MacBook right there at the diner table, fired up his terminal, and showed me Cucumber running plain-English acceptance tests. Before we left, he invited me to ChicagoRuby and told me about an open Friday pairing session happening up in Libertyville at a small consultancy called 8th Light.\n",
  "ACT 3: THE CORE INQUIRY: THE SOUND ABOVE [08:00 - 14:00]\nScene: 02. Monologue / Full Camera -> 04. Split Screen\n\nOur core inquiry for Episode 2 is simple: What makes a technical community endure across decades, and why does hospitality matter so much when engineers are crossing paradigm boundaries? When you are spending forty hours a week in a legacy stack, walking into a room full of people using a different operating system, a different editor, and a different language is intimidating. Ray understood that hospitality is not a soft extra. It is the bridge that lets working engineers cross over and learn new disciplines without feeling like outsiders.\n",
  "ACT 4: HALLWAY REWATCH & VIDEO REACTION [14:00 - 28:00]\nScene: 03. Reaction - Video Focus\n\nLet us roll the SCNA 2011 conversation with Ray Hightower (YouTube ID: XZqGbf5C76E). Pay attention to how intentionally Ray thinks about structure, venue logistics, and linking downtown city developers with suburban user groups across Chicagoland.\n",
  "ACT 5: THE AI-ERA PARALLEL & TERMINAL WORKBENCH [28:00 - 40:00]\nScene: 05. Workbench - Code & Terminal\n\nIn 2026, when so much engineering work happens in isolated remote home offices behind AI chat prompts, the risk of professional isolation is higher than it was in 2008. Autonomous code generation can synthesize boilerplate in seconds, but it cannot sit across from you at a diner table, show you a better testing workflow, or introduce you to the peers who will change the trajectory of your career. We still need human ambassadors and open rooms where engineers share their actual workbenches.\n",
  "ACT 6: COMMUNITY CODA, OUTRO & NEXT STREAM [40:00 - 45:00]\nScene: 08. Outro & Next Stream\n\nThank you for watching Episode 2 of The Sound Above. The full interactive transcript with Ray Hightower and our Chicago Community Timeline notes are live at just3ws.com. You can help underwrite our independent compute and archive work at patreon.com/whoistechcmty. Next time, in Episode 3, we follow Ray's introduction up to Libertyville for Pair Friday with Micah Martin and Mike Jansen of 8th Light. Until next time: keep your feedback loops fast, and your code humane.\n"
]